e': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-01T09:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tamir Kalifa and Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tamir', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kalifa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55da7777-f837-53dd-ba94-3907b476a6f0', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55da7777-f837-53dd-ba94-3907b476a6f0'}, {'abstract': 'The men might have been among the earliest to be stolen from their homeland and brought to the Americas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/01/science/africans-slavery-mexico-grave.html', 'snippet': 'The men might have been among the earliest to be stolen from their homeland and brought to the Americas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The three skulls were unlike hundreds of others in the 16th-century mass grave uncovered at the San José de los Naturales Royal Hospital in Mexico City. Their front teeth were filed decoratively, perhaps as a ritual custom, unlike those of “los naturales,” the Indigenous people who made up the majority of bodies at the colonial burial site. Archaeologists concluded the three individuals were most likely enslaved Africans, but they needed more evidence to be certain.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/26/science/01TB-SKELETONS2C/01TB-SKELETONS2C-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xla