on', 'byline': {'original': 'By George McGraw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGraw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/77229bb6-983e-5b24-b940-239195f174a3', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77229bb6-983e-5b24-b940-239195f174a3'}, {'abstract': 'After the coronavirus, political transformation may be inevitable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/02/opinion/sunday/coronavirus-new-deal-ubi.html', 'snippet': 'After the coronavirus, political transformation may be inevitable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until very recently, Andrew Yang thought that the need for a universal basic income would be a big issue in the 2024 election, as “many of the trends that I campaigned on were going to become completely clear to more and more Americans” over the next four years. He was arguing, for example, that between now and then, “30 percent of our stores and malls were going to close because of Amazon.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/02/opinion/02goldberg3/merlin_172112217_13c39e11-02c5-4fd4-a0c9-9848ccd70369-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/02/opinion/02goldberg3/merlin_172112217_13c39e11-02c5-4fd4-a0c9-9848ccd70369-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 472}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/02/opinion/02goldberg3/merlin_172112217_13c39e11-02c5-4fd4-a0c9-9848ccd70369-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 805, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':