cle', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Reader Center', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e8ed40-6a88-5493-aa43-dc910264c094', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e8ed40-6a88-5493-aa43-dc910264c094'}, {'abstract': 'It is just speeding up trends that were already underway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/03/opinion/coronavirus-economy-nationalism.html', 'snippet': 'It is just speeding up trends that were already underway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A crisis as life-altering as the coronavirus pandemic naturally inspires speculation about how it will change everything. But it is worth recalling that a far deadlier predecessor, the Spanish Flu, killed 50 million to 100 million people between 1918 and 1920, and was followed by the Roaring ’20s. So did it change anything?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/03/opinion/03sharma/03sharma-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 590, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/03/opinion/03sharma/03sharma-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 590}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/03/opinion/03sharma/03sharma-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1008, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/03/opinion/03sharma/03sharma-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2016, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank':