astname': 'Dosani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Westbrook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/2742d9ee-9b79-50ba-9ed8-1bc77fece82a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/2742d9ee-9b79-50ba-9ed8-1bc77fece82a'}, {'abstract': 'Gather couch cushions, pillows, chairs, sheets and blankets. Don’t get attached; feel free to renovate when needed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/05/magazine/how-to-build-a-fort.html', 'snippet': 'Gather couch cushions, pillows, chairs, sheets and blankets. Don’t get attached; feel free to renovate when needed.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Use flat sheets for the roof,” says Alexandra Feldner, 25, a third-year medical student at Rush Medical College in Chicago. At the end of March, after more than a week of sheltering in place in her small apartment, Feldner and her boyfriend spent an hour building an elaborate fort in her living room. They shot a time-lapse video of their construction process and uploaded it to the social media site TikTok, where it has been viewed 16.5 million times. Feldner figures the interest is owed to some combination of childhood nostalgia and a general longing for companionship and safety.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10Mag-Tip-01/10Mag-Tip-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10Mag-Tip-01/10Mag-Tip-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 530}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10Mag-Tip-01/10Mag-Tip-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 904, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10Mag-Tip-01/10Mag-Tip-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 917, 'width': 1039, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10Mag-Tip-01/10Mag-Tip-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10Mag-Tip-01/10Mag-Tip-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10Mag-Tip-01/10Mag-Tip-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Build a Fort', 'kicker': 'Tip ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Build a Fort', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pillows', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-05T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17d90325-728e-526b-b123-711ddfe54cfc', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17d90325-728e-526b-b123-711ddfe54cfc'}, {'abstract': 'First published in 1988, “Friend,” by Paek Nam-nyong, is a candid examination of domestic conflict and female ambition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/05/books/review/paek-nam-nyong-friend.html', 'snippet': 'First published in 1988, “Friend,” by Paek Nam-nyong, is a candid examination of domestic conflict and female ambition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It may be surprising, though it shouldn’t be, to learn that North Korea has novelists and literary critics, fiction prizes and best sellers. Some books have been republished in South Korea, but English translations remain scarce and geared largely toward dissident memoirs. “The Accusation,” an absorbing story collection by a man writing under the name Bandi (or “Firefly”), was published in English in 2017, but it never had a life inside North Korea: The stories were critical of the regime and had to be smuggled out of the country to be read.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/books/05RovingEye_Kim01/05RovingEye_Kim01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/17/books/05RovingEye_Kim01/05RovingEye_Kim01-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/books/05RovingEye_Kim01/05RovingEye_Kim01-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/books/05RovingEye_Kim01/05RovingEye_Kim01-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo'