: 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-05T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Mooney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mooney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba685109-c273-5fff-9ff0-6cfa94b024c0', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba685109-c273-5fff-9ff0-6cfa94b024c0'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the style of Dr. Amy Acton, who has earned praise for her daily briefings on the pandemic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/05/opinion/coronavirus-ohio-amy-acton.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the style of Dr. Amy Acton, who has earned praise for her daily briefings on the pandemic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The coronavirus has turned several public health officials and local leaders into bona fide celebrities, and perhaps no one is more compelling than the Ohio Health Department’s Dr. Amy Acton. She wasn’t just the brains behind the state’s early, aggressive coronavirus response; she was also its most effective messenger. In the video above, we deconstructed Dr. Acton’s daily briefings to find out why this previously unknown public health official now has her own Facebook fan club, T-shirts, chalk drawings and ’70s sitcom parodies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/autossell/86288_OP-ACTON_Thumb3/86288_OP-ACTON_Thumb3-articleLarge.png', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/05/autossell/86288_OP-ACTON_Thumb3/86288_OP-ACTON_Thumb3-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/autossell/86288_OP-ACTON_Thumb