stname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Westbrook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/98c3c999-4186-5225-9d66-4c8b0f571691', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98c3c999-4186-5225-9d66-4c8b0f571691'}, {'abstract': 'A simple circle, similar to the sundial, connects us to a time when the sky told humans how to live.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/05/magazine/how-analog-clocks-can-give-us-more-by-giving-us-less.html', 'snippet': 'A simple circle, similar to the sundial, connects us to a time when the sky told humans how to live.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My sister can’t read an analog clock. She’s 10 years younger than I am. No doubt that skill is disappearing from the populace, along with an avalanche of others: driving a stick shift, writing by hand, navigating by memory, using stamps. (I understood the world I knew was gone forever when I had to teach a college student how to operate an envelope.) I weep not for the stick shift and could never refold a map without making a crumpled mess, but I can’t imagine going through life without being able to read a clock.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag_LOR_AnalogClocks/10mag_LOR_AnalogClocks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag_LOR_AnalogClocks/10mag_LOR_AnalogClocks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag_LOR_AnalogClocks/10mag_LOR_AnalogClocks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 747, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}