uiet streets. Post-pandemic, might they do so again?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the media images that the Covid-19 crisis has generated in recent weeks, it is the city devoid of crowds that has perhaps been the most affecting. It doesn’t matter whether it’s New York, or Rome or London — it is the empty public space that most clearly signifies something is wrong. There ought to be crowds, and there aren’t. It is the classic horror movie trope. Closer to home, it is what most disturbs and compels us about contemporary Detroit — except we are all Detroiters now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/opinion/05Williams3/05Williams3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/05/opinion/05Williams3/05Williams3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/opinion/05Williams3/05Williams3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/opinion/05Williams3/05Williams3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1388, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/opinion/05Williams3/05Williams3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/05/opinion/05Williams3/05Williams3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/opinion/05Williams3/05Williams3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will You Want to Go Straight Back Into the Crowd?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Le Corbusier', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Barcelona (Spain)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brasilia (Brazil)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manchester (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-05T19:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard J. Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a46aaeba-edec-51bc-8a16-6877d27f581b', 'word_count': 1354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a46aaeba-edec-51bc-8a16-6877d27f581b'}, {'abstract': 'I’m a New York City subway conductor who had Covid-19. Now I’m going back to work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/05/opinion/coronavirus-nyc-subway.html', 'snippet': 'I’m a New York City subway conductor who had Covid-19. Now I’m going back to work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I heard that a co-worker had died from Covid-19 — the first in the Metropolitan Transportation Authority — on March 27, I thought, “It’s starting.” More deaths followed in quick succession, frequently more than once a day. Some of those people I used to see every day and fist bump.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/05gidla1/merlin_170879556_295b0d35-d526-4f3c-a23d-ae705853016a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/05gidla1/merlin_170879556_295b0d35-d526-4f3c-a23d-ae705853016a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/05gidla1/merlin_170879556_295b0d35-d526-4f3c-a23d-ae705853016a-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/05gidla1/merlin_170879556_295b0d35-d526-4f3c-a23d-ae705853016a-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/05gidla1/05gidla1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/05gidla1/05gidla1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/05gidla1/05gidla1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘We Are Not Essential. We Are Sacrificial.’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Essential, and Sacrificed to Covid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Hazards and Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Protective Clothing and Gear', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Masks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-05T19:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sujatha Gidla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sujatha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gidla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1ea19c6-d6f5-5b90-8b0d-f9a40e755c2f', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1ea19c6-d6f5-5b90-8b0d-f9a40e755c2f'}, {'abstract': 'Modern Love in miniature, featuring reader-submitted stories of no more than 100 words.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/05/style/tiny-modern-love-stories-coronavirus-cute-smile-funny-caption.html', 'snippet': 'Modern Love in miniature, featuring reader-submitted stories of no more than 100 words.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two dots each, mine vertical and my sister’s horizontal. When Shuchi and I were growing up, our mother used to mark “our dots” on our underwear so she could tell them apart in the laundry. Now we have them tattooed on our wrists — not to keep us apart, but to show that we are always together. — Richa Chaturvedi', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/fashion/05TINYLOVE-ILLO1/05TINYLOVE-ILLO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'sub