, 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gabe Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65750efd-c1fb-5326-a98d-020cb213f65f', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65750efd-c1fb-5326-a98d-020cb213f65f'}, {'abstract': 'Anger over a failing economy and unaffordable food has pushed protesters into the streets despite the coronavirus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/06/opinion/lebanon-protests-coronavirus.html', 'snippet': 'Anger over a failing economy and unaffordable food has pushed protesters into the streets despite the coronavirus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Two weeks ago, it seemed every conversation in Lebanon was about keeping safe from the virus. The bustling streets of Beirut were quiet; everyone wore masks and gloves and glared at anyone who coughed in public. The smell of hand sanitizers filled elevators.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06mounzer1/merlin_172012008_11da384c-a348-41b8-af73-1fcd9b653da5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06mounzer1/merlin_172012008_11da384c-a348-41b8-af73-1fcd9b653da5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06mounzer1/merlin_172012008_11da384c-a348-41b8-af73-1fcd9b653da5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opini