'The Future of Travel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruises', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-06T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac, Tariro Mzezewa and Sarah Firshein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tariro', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mzezewa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firshein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/419127db-05d6-5886-96eb-5601999d0d40', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/419127db-05d6-5886-96eb-5601999d0d40'}, {'abstract': 'Stars seem unsure what they should be doing right now. The “One World” broadcast offered one answer: Tell us what we already believe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/06/magazine/whats-the-point-of-a-celebrity-in-a-pandemic.html', 'snippet': 'Stars seem unsure what they should be doing right now. The “One World” broadcast offered one answer: Tell us what we already believe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jimmy Fallon’s appeal, such as it is, resides in his air of benighted, puppyish cluelessness — but in times of crisis, this sort of thing can quickly lose its charm. Fallon himself seemed to learn this in 2016, after he chummily tousled Donald Trump’s hair during a “Tonight Show” appearance, sparking a public reaction so negative the host was pushed into a rare sulk. “What do you want me to do? You want me to kill myself?” he asked in The Hollywood Reporter. “I’m sorry. I don’t want to make anyone angry — I never do, and I never will. It’s all in the fun of the show.” Here he had a point. Jimmy Fallon is not like Stephen Colbert or Trevor Noah or John Oliver or Samantha Bee. His role is not to engage, however wryly, with real life. It is to show up every night and distract us from it, as though it’s not really there.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-screenland-1/10mag-screenland-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-screenland-1/10mag-screenland-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-screenland-1/10mag-screenland-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-screenland-1/10mag-screenland-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-screenland-1/10mag-screenland-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-screenland-1/10mag-screenland-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-screenland-1/10mag-screenland-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s the Point of a Celebrity in a Pandemic?', 'kicker': 'Screenland', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can Do', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hygiene and Cleanliness', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colbert, Stephen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fallon, Jimmy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bee, Samantha', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Saturday Night Live (TV Program)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Daily Show with Trevor Noah (TV Program)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-06T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carina Chocano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chocano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63ec9b28-9ece-5a8e-9ee4-5b25b7692b92', 'word_count': 1139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63ec9b28-9ece-5a8e-9ee4-5b25b7692b92'}, {'abstract': 'In New York City and around the country, community health centers — which serve vulnerable, hard-to-reach populations — are being strained like never before.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/06/magazine/community-health-centers-covid.html', 'snippet': 'In New York City and around the country, community health centers — which serve vulnerable, hard-to-reach populations — are being strained like never before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March, New York City began moving homeless shelter residents believed to have Covid-19 to “isolation units” within existing facilities. In April, it began using the city’s inventory of empty hotels, which were supposed to be for residents who weren’t yet sick enough to need hospital care. There was plenty of space available; the problem was how to staff it. For one hotel, the city contracted with Housing Works, a nonprofit focused on homelessness and H.I.V./AIDS. Housing Works brought in Callen-Lorde Community Health Center, which serves L.G.B.T.Q. New Yorkers. Callen-Lorde’s staff learned the hotel’s exact location, in Queens, late in the morning on Friday, April 3. There were 133 rooms, expected to hold more than 170 patients. They had only a few hours to get the place ready: The first patients would begin arriving that night.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-lorde/10mag-lorde-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-lorde/10mag-lorde-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/magazine/10mag-lorde/10mag-lorde-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'