'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Hirshman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirshman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dc94276-6907-5a60-8c46-eecbf6133e5a', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dc94276-6907-5a60-8c46-eecbf6133e5a'}, {'abstract': 'As a war veteran, I know that trauma survivors can emerge with a deeper, richer appreciation of life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/06/opinion/coronavirus-ptsd-recovery.html', 'snippet': 'As a war veteran, I know that trauma survivors can emerge with a deeper, richer appreciation of life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My wife teases me sometimes. She calls me Sobchak, as in Walter Sobchak, John Goodman’s character in the film “The Big Lebowski,” the veteran who equates everything with Vietnam. My wars were Iraq and Afghanistan, and life during the coronavirus pandemic does keep reminding me of them. The way each day bleeds into the next. The threat that stalks outside. The surrealness of it all. Before heading to the grocery store, as we inspect our masks and lay out sets of rubber gloves, I tell my wife that our meticulous preparation feels like precombat checks before a patrol. When we’re in the grocery store and she reminds me to keep six feet apart, I tell her she sounds like my old platoon sergeant reminding Marines to check their “fives and twenty-fives,” the meter distances from the road where insurgents typically plant I.E.D.s.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06ackerman1/merlin_171910341_d53695e6-06fd-454e-a8dc-f54ac64c60e4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06ackerman1/merlin_171910341_d53695e6-06fd-454e-a8dc-f54ac64c60e4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06ackerman1/merlin_171910341_d53695e6-06fd-454e-a8dc-f54ac64c60e4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06ackerman1/merlin_171910341_d53695e6-06fd-454e-a8dc-f54ac64c60e4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06ackerman1/06ackerman1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06ackerman1/06ackerman1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/06/opinion/06ackerman1/06ackerman1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Our Nation Grieves, It Can Grow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grief (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-06T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elliot Ackerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elliot', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ackerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1da6d47-d259-50da-865f-790f6c10f34a', 'word_count': 1333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1da6d47-d259-50da-865f-790f6c10f34a'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a list of coronavirus symptoms that many can now recite from memory. And then there’s how it actually feels when you have it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/article/coronavirus-symptoms.html', 'snippet': 'There’s a list of coronavirus symptoms that many can now recite from memory. And then there’s how it actually feels when you have it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a clinical list of Covid-19 symptoms that includes a dry cough, a fever and shortness of breath. And then there is how the disease actually feels. It is like a lengthy hangover. An anvil on your chest. An alien takeover. It is like being in a fight with Mike Tyson.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘An Anvil Sitting on My Chest’: What It’s Like to Have Covid-19', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘An Anvil Sitting On My Chest’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fever', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coughs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ventilators (Medical)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Medical Treatment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantines', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-06T09:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times and Thoka Maer', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 