': '2020-05-07T18:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fahim Abed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fahim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4168e076-6abd-50d3-a549-b864d9911d2c', 'word_count': 4691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4168e076-6abd-50d3-a549-b864d9911d2c'}, {'abstract': 'The jumpin’ jive glories of old Hollywood musicals lift us out of the everyday one tap, leap and irresistible beat at a time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/07/movies/1940s-musicals.html', 'snippet': 'The jumpin’ jive glories of old Hollywood musicals lift us out of the everyday one tap, leap and irresistible beat at a time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Musicals are my greatest, most reliable cinematic refuge, a warm blanket and hot toddy combined. Even on a bad day — and we’ve all had plenty of those lately — a musical can lift my spirits if only for a moment. Their otherworldliness is crucial because while all fiction depends on some kind of contract between creators and audiences, musicals also rely on us agreeing to voyage beyond consensus reality. No matter how far-fetched the premise or gossamer-thin the story, the musical invites (compels) us to go along with its essential surrealism, to travel to that dream space where everyday life suddenly moves and sounds deliriously out of this world.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Escape With Me Into the Delirious World of 1940s Musicals', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Great Escape, Try a 1940s Musical', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantine (Life and Culture)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Meet Me in St. Louis (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'On the Town (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Stormy Weather (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "You'll Never Get Rich (Movie)", 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Down Argentine Way (Movie)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Forties', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-07T18:16:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/586339e8-b036-571c-8848-3ba21ecc48fa', 'word_count': 1294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/586339e8-b036-571c-8848-3ba21ecc48fa'}, {'abstract': 'The defeat of the measure allows President Trump to continue to act unilaterally on Iran, in defiance of Congress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/07/us/politics/senate-war-powers-trump.html', 'snippet': 'The defeat of the measure allows President Trump to continue to act unilaterally on Iran, in defiance of Congress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Senate failed on Thursday to overturn President Trump’s veto of a resolution seeking to block him from taking further military action against Iran without explicit approval from Congress, falling short in its latest effort to curtail his unilateral moves on matters of war and peace.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xl