{'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6c95e76-77ae-50bc-bfe4-41cc369d38b5', 'word_count': 1387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6c95e76-77ae-50bc-bfe4-41cc369d38b5'}, {'abstract': 'I wish someone had told me about the beauty that waited at the end of this journey.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/09/opinion/sunday/losing-my-mom-to-alzheimers-then-finding-her-again.html', 'snippet': 'I wish someone had told me about the beauty that waited at the end of this journey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I lost my mother at about this time last year, after her 10-year struggle with Alzheimer’s disease. During this past year, kind friends have checked in at holidays and birthdays, understandably assuming these first landmark occasions without my mom would be difficult. The truth is something that I was at first ashamed to admit, let alone articulate: Grieving her now, when the loss is complete, feels nothing like I had expected. It feels easy.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/opinion/sunday/10morgan/10morgan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/10/opinion/sunday/10morgan/10morgan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/opinion/sunday/10morgan/10morgan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/10/opinion/