ork on this issue, the world was another way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/11/t-magazine/silk-road-travel-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Last May, when our known world was one way, we began planning these stories. By last month, when we were finishing work on this issue, the world was another way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LIKE OUR JOURNEY from the womb, or through millennia of evolutionary changes, the Silk Road is a trip that feels elemental to the human condition. We cannot remember, often, how we learned of it, only that knowledge of it seems to be a birthright: It may not be the first story of human movement, recorded or otherwise, but it is the most abiding.', 'print_section': 'P2', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-maps/17tmag-maps-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-maps/17tmag-maps-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-maps/17tmag-maps-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-maps/17tmag-maps-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1089, 'width': 1548, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-maps/17tmag-maps-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-maps/17tmag-maps-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-maps/17tmag-maps-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Silk Road’s Enduring Romance, and Eternal Influence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Way Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central Asia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silk Road (Ancient Trade Route)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-11T13:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hanya Yanagihara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yanagihara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e88cecb4-afd0-5e25-bb4e-9fdab05797e8', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e88cecb4-afd0-5e25-bb4e-9fdab05797e8'}, {'abstract': 'The sumptuous craft was introduced along the country’s trade routes centuries ago. Even now, links to this delicate tradition still remain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/11/t-magazine/silk-road-tbilisi-georgia.html', 'snippet': 'The sumptuous craft was introduced along the country’s trade routes centuries ago. Even now, links to this delicate tradition still remain.', 'lead_paragraph': '1. TBILISI RISES OUT of the steep banks of the Mtkvari, in Georgia, spread wide on either side of the river like hands cupping a bowl — a city of cobblestones, winding lanes and wrought-iron gates bleeding rust. It is filled with the particular decay of the old Soviet republics, and the air smells of fresh bread, hot herb soup and scorched motor oil.', 'print_section': 'P2', 'print_page': '75', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 459, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 459}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1568, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK/17tmag-tbilisi-slide-J8LK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Silk-Making Represents a More Hidden Side of Georgia’s Past', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Single Thread', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tbilisi (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silk Road (Ancient Trade Route)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-11T13:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Esi Edugyan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Esi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edugyan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4aca5f2e-42da-5235-a2dd-fcd894258671', 'word_count': 3139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4aca5f2e-42da-5235-a2dd-fcd894258671'}, {'abstract': 'His image is so commonplace that you could believe it must always have existed — yet for six centuries after his death, he was never once depicted in human form.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/11/t-magazine/buddha-statues-face.html', 'snippet': 'His image is so commonplace that you could believe it must always have existed — yet for six centuries after his death, he was never once depicted in human form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WE FLEW THROUGH the thin, light-suffused mist of a December afternoon in north India before landing among open fields outside the paramount site of Hindu pilgrimage: Varanasi, a temple town that curls around the Ganges, the equivalent of Rome or Jerusalem in the Hindu imagination. But the pilgrims on my flight from South Korea had an altogether different purpose. It was here, scarcely 15 miles from the airport, among fields now yellow with mustard flowers, that a renunciant prince had, upon gaining enlightenment some 25 centuries ago, given his first sermon, setting what Buddhists call the Wheel of Dharma into motion. At a deer park once called Isipatana, now Sarnath, a 35-year-old Gautama Buddha, hardly older than Christ when he climbed the hill of Calvary, revealed the eightfold path to liberation from suffering, his four noble truths and the doctrine of the impermanence of everything, including the Self. It was to the remains of the monastery and shrine at Sarnath that the pilgrims from East and Southeast Asia came, as pilgrims had for well over 1,500 years, along a subsidiary branch of the Great Silk Road, which ran through the high snowy mountains that girdle the Indian subcontinent into a riverine plain that stretches across what is today Pakistan and north India. The pilgrims took an exit off that highway of goods and ideas that ran from China to Rome in order to honor what may well have been the most influential doctrine to travel along its lines of transmission — the word of the Buddha, and the art made in his name.', 'print_section': 'P2', 'print_page': '57', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 476, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 476}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1261, 'width': 1591, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3/17tmag-sarnath-slide-HXV3-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Buddha Got His Face', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How the Buddha Got His Face', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buddhism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pilgrimages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monasteries and Monks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nomadic People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silk Road (Ancient Trade Route)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Varanasi (India)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-11T13:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aatish Taseer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aatish', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taseer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcba1772-6aa9-59a0-93d6-f8ae48d9c5e5', 'word_count': 4129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcba1772-6aa9-59a0-93d6-f8ae48d9c5e5'}, {'abstract': 'Central Asia was once home to several bustling trade cities. Today, traveling through them reawakens a distant, though not forgotten, past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/11/t-magazine/uzbekistan-history-silk-road.html', 'snippet': 'Central Asia was once home to several bustling trade cities. Today, traveling through them reawakens a distant, though not forgotten, past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT WAS OCTOBER in Tashkent. The broad Soviet-style avenues of Uzbekistan’s capital were lined with chestnut and Oriental plane, their leaves turning russet in the crisp autumn air. This city of 2.5 million had, in Soviet days, which lasted from the 1920s until the country’s independence in 1991, been the premier capital of Central Asia. It is home to more than half of Uzbekistan’s 116 universities, and on that first golden morning in Tashkent, there was something of the glazed perfection of a Soviet propaganda poster in the sight of students in twos and threes strolling down the runway-size avenues. They were dwarfed by the giant buildings that lined the roads — banks, museums and ministries — “Babylonian blocks,” as the English writer Philip Glazebrook, who had been in Tashkent at the end of Soviet rule, described them in “Journey to Khiva” in the early ’90s: “Since the days of Nineveh this has been the architecture of dictatorship and persecution.” And so it was, but after my late arrival on the Turkish Airlines flight from Istanbul, I found myself oddly in sympathy with the ideal, if not the reality, of Soviet life.', 'print_section': 'P2', 'print_page': '65', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/t-magazine/17tmag-uzbekistan-slide-LP4D/17tmag-uzbekistan-slide-LP4D-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 479, 