, 'byline': {'original': 'By David Marchese', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marchese', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/c624cbe3-dc52-5a79-b1d5-3133f8995df5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/c624cbe3-dc52-5a79-b1d5-3133f8995df5'}, {'abstract': 'There is new evidence that some children may become very sick, and we are beginning to learn more about who may be most at risk and what parents need to watch for.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/12/well/family/coronavirus-children-covid-19.html', 'snippet': 'There is new evidence that some children may become very sick, and we are beginning to learn more about who may be most at risk and what parents need to watch for.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As we learn more about children and Covid-19, new research is reshaping some of our thinking. It continues to be true that children, as a group, have been relatively spared, but there is evidence that some may become very sick, and we are beginning to learn more about who may be most at risk, and what parents need to watch for.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/01/well/12klass-print/merlin_172386213_32d180a3-8fdd-4227-ae3f-de847cb70a3d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/01/well/12klass-print/merlin_172386213_32d180a3-8fdd-4227-ae3f-de847cb70a3d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 419}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/01/well/12klass-print/merlin_172386213_32d180a3-8fdd-4227-ae3f-de847cb70a3d-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'le