': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lambert, David Eric', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-12T18:54:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Express', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Cramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28aedd44-6a54-5ca1-99fb-1a12802337e0', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28aedd44-6a54-5ca1-99fb-1a12802337e0'}, {'abstract': 'Modern Love in miniature, featuring reader-submitted stories of no more than 100 words.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/12/style/tiny-modern-love-stories-coronavirus-me-looking-at-you.html', 'snippet': 'Modern Love in miniature, featuring reader-submitted stories of no more than 100 words.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you were 18 and I was 17, and I decided to leave high school, my grandmother asked for a picture of me for her bookshelf. She wanted a portrait to place next to the framed photos of her other grandchildren, all smiling in caps and gowns. I gave her the picture that you had taken in the field behind my house, cropped to hide my unbuttoned dress, my splotchy chest. Although our relationship didn’t last, for the next 30 years I had to look at a picture of me looking at you. — Bethany Dorau', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/fashion/05TINYLOVE-ILLO3/05TINYLOVE-ILLO3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/05/fashion/05TINYLOVE-ILLO3/05TINYLOVE-ILLO3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/05/fashion/05