d', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalia Mehlman Petrzela', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalia', 'middlename': 'Mehlman', 'lastname': 'Petrzela', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9bfd72b-9ef5-50a1-afd4-b65fcbf0c008', 'word_count': 1110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9bfd72b-9ef5-50a1-afd4-b65fcbf0c008'}, {'abstract': 'An analysis by The New York Times found some cities in Latin America have seen spikes in fatalities that match the worst of the pandemic elsewhere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/12/world/americas/latin-america-virus-death.html', 'snippet': 'An analysis by The New York Times found some cities in Latin America have seen spikes in fatalities that match the worst of the pandemic elsewhere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deaths doubled in Lima, rivaling the worst month of the pandemic in Paris. They tripled in Manaus, a metropolis tucked deep in Brazil’s Amazon — a surge similar to what London and Madrid endured.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/12/world/12virus-latam-ES-01/merlin_171884274_4780dc79-db68-4fcd-9c89-00def7bbd158-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/12/world/12virus-latam-ES-01/merlin_171884274_4780dc79-db68-4fcd-9c89-00def7bbd158-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/12/world/12virus-latam-ES-01/merlin_171884274_4780dc79-db68-4fcd-9c89-00def7bbd158-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': No