d_count': 843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f037021-4567-57d5-b18f-042cb8b032ae'}, {'abstract': 'Cultural hosts Lu Zhang and Herb Tam, “Corona Daze” poetry, printable exhibitions by Tiger Strikes Asteroid, Google street views.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/13/arts/design/artists-follow-instagram-coronavirus-quarantine.html', 'snippet': 'Cultural hosts Lu Zhang and Herb Tam, “Corona Daze” poetry, printable exhibitions by Tiger Strikes Asteroid, Google street views.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a confession: I find looking at art on a computer to be generally unsatisfying. It’s no one’s fault, really, but digital images don’t capture the complexity of seeing a work in person, especially if it’s a three-dimensional object. The quality of my attention is different, too. Whereas I can spend several hours visiting galleries with only sporadic checks of my phone, I’m prone to refresh my email every five minutes while trying to view anything in a browser — especially now, when the pandemic has heightened my anxiety and distraction.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/15/arts/14instagram-artists1/merlin_172370235_79189894-9265-4a27-812c-8bc73add89e7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/15/arts/14instagram-artists1/merlin_172370235_79189894-9265-4a27-812c-8bc73add89e7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/15/arts/14instagram-artists1/merlin_172370235_79189894-9265-4a27-812c-8bc73add89e7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20