'word_count': 2111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e429beae-5cf8-5e41-b392-26ca10555f2d'}, {'abstract': 'A poem that opens hands and heart to a reader — no double talk or cryptic shadings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/14/magazine/poem-i-have-tried-hard-to-have-appropriate-feelings.html', 'snippet': 'A poem that opens hands and heart to a reader — no double talk or cryptic shadings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Susan Leslie Moore’s poem from “That Place Where You Opened Your Hands” instantly offers forthright presence: a personal voice making honest admissions. It’s a poem that opens hands and heart to a reader — no double talk or cryptic shadings. Including a turn on a familiar song line, a spatial obsession and that disquieting image of stranded polar bears, then the very original cure of being “kept in line” by something familiar outside the window, this poem suits our time of roller-coastering moods. Selected by Naomi Shihab Nye', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 228, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 228}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/02/17/magazine/17mag-poem-image1/17mag-poem-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 1479, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag