 'nyt://article/437469e7-cb47-5c45-aa4c-184eb00cc44b'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s Crime column includes a computer gamer who is handy with a Samurai sword and a 14-year-old girl who solves a murder in an amusement park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/15/books/review/crime-fiction-victim-2117-jussi-adler-olsen.html', 'snippet': 'This week’s Crime column includes a computer gamer who is handy with a Samurai sword and a 14-year-old girl who solves a murder in an amusement park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Danish crime writer Jussi Adler-Olsen doesn’t fool around. If he wants to creep you out, he knows exactly how to do it. Take this passage from his latest Department Q novel, VICTIM 2117 (Dutton, 480 pp., $28), in which the villain torments a man named Assad by sending him a message about his abducted wife, Marwa. “It said that he made sure Marwa lost our third child,” Assad discloses, “and that he raped Marwa and my daughters every day, and that every time they gave birth, he immediately killed the child.” Has a certain sadistic ring to it, doesn’t it? That’s how we know we are indeed reading Adler-Olsen, whose style might best be described as literary brutalism.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/books/review/17Crime/17Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1052, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/17/books/review/17Crime/17Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1052}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/books/review/17Crime/17Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 585, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17