h Vowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf81981e-a5f7-5650-ba20-5b4702587ec7', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf81981e-a5f7-5650-ba20-5b4702587ec7'}, {'abstract': 'Being at home with family has made observing Islam’s holiest month a richer and deeper spiritual experience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/17/opinion/ramadan-coronavirus-quarantine.html', 'snippet': 'Being at home with family has made observing Islam’s holiest month a richer and deeper spiritual experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Gov. Andrew Cuomo of New York announced that lockdown would be extended through May 15, I wondered how this would affect Ramadan. After all, dinner parties and communal prayers have long been integral to this month, which began on April 24. But rather unexpectedly, quarantine has enhanced my Ramadan experience. Being at home has made it easier to observe our holiest month and made it a richer and deeper spiritual affair.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/opinion/17Abedi1/17Abedi1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/17/opinion/17Abedi1/17Abedi1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/opinion/17Abedi1/17Abedi1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/17/opinion/17Abedi1/17Abedi1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 