-19T09:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/25fdbe52-7159-5786-9e93-147fba620267', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25fdbe52-7159-5786-9e93-147fba620267'}, {'abstract': 'An excerpt from “The Equivalents,” by Maggie Doherty', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/19/books/review/the-equivalents-by-maggie-doherty-an-excerpt.html', 'snippet': 'An excerpt from “The Equivalents,” by Maggie Doherty', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the fall of 1960, the prestigious women’s college Radcliffe, the sister school of Harvard, had announced an unprecedented fellowship program, one that targeted a ubiquitous and yet marginalized class of Americans: mothers. In the words of its founder, the Radcliffe president and microbiologist Mary Ingraham Bunting, the Radcliffe Institute for Independent Study was designed to combat the “climate of unexpectation” facing women in mid-century America. As she saw it, too many accomplished female undergraduates were giving up their dreams of becoming scholars or artists because they couldn’t see how they could do research or write a book while also managing a family and keeping house. The new program proposed to get these “intellectually displaced women” back on track.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Equivalents,’ by Maggie Doherty: An Excerpt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2020-05-19T09:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ab50fca-a767-5c7b-b756-e89ed5d9d2f4', 'word_count': 1488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ab50fca-a767-5c7b-b756-e89ed5d9d2f4'}, {'abstract': 'An excerpt from “Drifts,” by Kate Zambreno', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/19/books/review/drifts-by-kate-zambreno-an-excerpt.html', 'snippet': 'An excerpt from “Drifts,” by Kate Zambreno', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 2015, I was supposed to be at work on Drifts, a book I had been under contract for almost as long as I had lived in this city, renting the first floor of a shabby Victorian house in a tree-lined neighborhood so remote it was almost a suburb. The title of the book came from a feeling, and I wanted to write through this feeling. What I really wanted to write was my present tense, which seemed impossible. How can a paragraph be a day, or a day a paragraph? But I couldn’t often exist in the room, or even in this paragraph, now. I found myself always distracted.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Drifts,’ by Kate Zambreno: An Excerpt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2020-05-19T09:01:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a32b931c-2fed-58fe-b0e9-5456afd50e9b', 'word_count': 2428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a32b931c-2fed-58fe-b0e9-5456afd50e9b'}, {'abstract': 'More changes took effect on Wednesday, but vast discrepancies remain as states forge ahead after shutdowns. Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin warns of “permanent damage” to the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/19/us/coronavirus-usa-live.html', 'snippet': 'More changes took effect on Wednesday, but vast discrepancies remain as states forge ahead after shutdowns. Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin warns of “permanent damage” to the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This 