s_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Mather', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mather', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77e8b2b7-7f0b-5f50-9d17-91a1347c87fb', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77e8b2b7-7f0b-5f50-9d17-91a1347c87fb'}, {'abstract': 'The 24-year-old’s single “My Truck” is the most viable fusion of rural and urban since “Old Town Road,” and his new EP pushes the sound even further.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/20/arts/music/breland-country-rap-my-truck.html', 'snippet': 'The 24-year-old’s single “My Truck” is the most viable fusion of rural and urban since “Old Town Road,” and his new EP pushes the sound even further.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the video for Breland’s breakout single, “My Truck,” the twangy sung intro is mouthed by a stoic Marlboro Man type. But then comes Breland, wiry and enthusiastic, shoving him out of the frame to take over the singing, providing an object lesson in genre and racial expectations. “My Truck,” one of the year’s most beguiling singles, is a fluent amalgam of country verbiage and vocal texture with hip-hop bluster and cadence. “Wood-grain dash with the matte-black finish,” Breland sing-raps, “and it match my shawty with the big ol’ butt.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/20/arts/20breland1/20breland1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/20/arts/20breland1/20breland1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 801}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/20/arts/20breland1/20breland1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/20/arts/20breland1/20breland1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1535, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/20/arts/20breland1/20breland1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/05/20/arts/20breland1/20breland1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/20/arts/20breland1/20breland1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Breland Is Country. Breland Is Hip-Hop. Can Breland Go Pop?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Twangy Hip-Hop? He’s All In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'RHYTHM AND BLUES (MUSIC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Country Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunt, Sam (1984- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breland, Daniel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'My Truck (Song)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-20T15:53:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'