tion': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Denise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa7db214-734a-547a-8188-49a270d30ddb', 'word_count': 1855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa7db214-734a-547a-8188-49a270d30ddb'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon keep a low profile, but they run the show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/23/crosswords/variety-acrostic.html', 'snippet': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon keep a low profile, but they run the show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ACROSTIC — This is a classic Team Cox-Rathvon acrostic where a topic makes itself evident in the clues as well as in the grid, as you tease the whole thing apart — familiar territory, but still satisfying. The passage is from an 1899 book (with a gorgeous gilded Art Nouveau cover) by Mabel Wagnalls, called “Stars of the Opera,” that lionizes everyone, including the lead sopranos and the occupants of the orchestra pit. I love it when professionals who operate in the background receive proper attention, and I find that the statements made about the opera’s orchestra apply to great support staff everywhere.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: Acrostic', 'kicker': 'Sunday Variety Column', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cox, Emily (Crossword Constructor)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rathvon, Henry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-23T15:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Games', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Lovinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc8c826b-6dd5-5b92-838f-647d91a1bb93', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc8c826b-6dd5-5b92-838f-647d91a1bb93'}, {'abstract': 'The city is reviled for its sprawl-and-crawl culture, but a pandemic can change things.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/23/opinion/coronavirus-los-angeles-cars.html', 'snippet': 'The city is reviled for its sprawl-and-crawl culture, but a pandemic can change things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — I was born in New York and spent my formative years there, so waiting makes me crazy. I’m a jaywalker and I push elevator buttons more than once. I find the shortest line to anything. I walk fast and can drive like a competitor in a video game race. I attribute these qualities to years spent trying to survive in New York’s hustle culture, and not to innate personal flaws.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/25/opinion/23wilentz/23wilentz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/05/25/opinion/23wilentz/23wilentz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/25/opinion/23wilentz/23wilentz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1013, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/25/opinion/23wilentz/23wilentz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2026, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image