 None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2020-05-26T04:47:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabella Kwai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabella', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kwai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ab6be19-d248-5c12-9ac6-deea5ee07244', 'word_count': 1485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ab6be19-d248-5c12-9ac6-deea5ee07244'}, {'abstract': 'The Australian comedian returns with a highly anticipated Netflix special, and “America’s Got Talent” is back for a 15th season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/26/arts/television/whats-on-tv-tuesday-hannah-gadsby-americas-got-talent.html', 'snippet': 'The Australian comedian returns with a highly anticipated Netflix special, and “America’s Got Talent” is back for a 15th season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HANNAH GADSBY: DOUGLAS (2020) Stream on Netflix. The Australian comedian Hannah Gadsby burst onto the world stage in 2018 with “Nanette,” a Netflix special that challenged what stand-up could or should be. She even vowed to give up comedy, at least as a tool she had long used to confront trauma. In this follow-up special, named after one of her dogs, Gadsby discusses her autism diagnosis, goes after a long list of targets — particularly men and centuries-old forms of patriarchy — and delivers more self-deprecating jokes. If she knew how popular “Nanette” was going to be, she says, she would have paced herself instead of putting all her “trauma eggs into one basket.” When Gadsby performed “Douglas” Off Broadway last summer, The New York Times’s theater critic Jesse Green named it a Critic’s Pick and wrote, “‘Douglas’ is angry — even furious — and draws its comic energy from the effort to master and direct that anger into sharp insight and its byproduct, laughter.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 