'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Herbert, Frank', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dune (Book)', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-26T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98a715db-0674-50ae-80e3-f0bf14fe896b', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98a715db-0674-50ae-80e3-f0bf14fe896b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Fairest,” Meredith Talusan looks back on her life lived at the intersection of genders, races, sexualities and cultures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/26/books/review/fairest-meredith-talusan.html', 'snippet': 'In “Fairest,” Meredith Talusan looks back on her life lived at the intersection of genders, races, sexualities and cultures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The journalist Meredith Talusan was a young teenager in a small town in the Philippines when, in 1989, the legal scholar Kimberlé Crenshaw proposed the concept of intersectionality to describe the ways in which race and gender and sexuality overlap to either amplify or ease discrimination. Decades later, as social media has catapulted academic jargon into everyday vocabulary, the term has become warped to signify a restructuring of society, with minorities at the top. But still this theory — that identity categories are not mutually exclusive, but integrated components of a single, multidimensional experience — remains immensely relevant today. Talusan’s debut memoir, “Fairest,” embodies both Crenshaw’s original framework as well as its contemporary misunderstanding, rendering an intellectual debate intimate.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/05/31/books/review/31Kameir1/Kameir1-articleLarge.jpg