nk': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Space Exploration Technologies Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Behnken, Robert L', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurley, Douglas G', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space and Astronomy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Musk, Elon', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-30T14:05:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/786d7923-535a-51f6-8e6a-fb91af476167', 'word_count': 3107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/786d7923-535a-51f6-8e6a-fb91af476167'}, {'abstract': 'In Katherine Applegate’s “The One and Only Bob,” Ivan’s best buddy strikes out on his own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/30/books/review/katherine-applegate-the-one-and-only-bob.html', 'snippet': 'In Katherine Applegate’s “The One and Only Bob,” Ivan’s best buddy strikes out on his own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Outsider stories resonate strongly with children: Even the luckiest of us start life in a land of giants where doorknobs and cookie jars hover beyond reach and adults call most of the shots. Katherine Applegate has made a specialty of mining this emotional territory in novels that blend elements of fantasy and realism while tracking the changing fortunes of resilient outsiders — like a homeless fifth grader (“Crenshaw”) and a Sudanese refugee (“Home of the Brave”), or, most memorably to date, a silverback gorilla living in captivity as the star attraction of a squalid shopping-mall tourist destination (“The One and Only Ivan”).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS/07-BKS-MARCUS_KIDS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Fans of Ivan, the Answer to ‘What About Bob?’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The One and Only Bob (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Applegate, Katherine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2020-05-30T14:26:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leonard S. Marcus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leonard', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Marcus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/00bec74c-43b3-5dde-9702-8352883f267e', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00bec74c-43b3-5dde-9702-8352883f267e'}, {'abstract': 'In William Shivering’s “Thieves of Weirdwood,” a mirror city’s buildings and streets physically reflect the hopes and fears of a “normal,” grim, Dickensian city’s residents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2020/05/30/books/review/thieves-of-weirdwood-christian-mckay-heidicker.html', 'snippet': 'In William Shivering’s “Thieves of Weirdwood,” a mirror city’s buildings and streets physically reflect the hopes and fears of a “normal,” grim, Dickensian city’s residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most important thing to know about William Shivering’s THIEVES OF WEIRDWOOD (Holt, 338 pp., $16.99; ages 8 to 12) is that it will delight and satiate those besotted with Harry Potter, Percy Jackson, Artemis Fowl, Miss Peregrine, the Spiderwicks. It boasts a familiar but enticing list of ingredients — an orphaned hero, plucky sidekicks, shape-shifting monsters, hidden worlds, magical peacekeepers, foul deeds, fateful destinies — whisked into a story that is fast-paced, funny and sometimes surprising. It comes to a satisfying conclusion, though with just enough loose ends, and an appended cliffhanger, to propel readers forward to the next installment, since being the first book in a series is arguably the single most essential part of the recipe, like sugar in a cookie.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/06/07/books/review/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS/07-BKS-HANDY_KIDS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tale of Twinned Cities', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, 